  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "What is the probability that among $3$ randomly chosen people, all born on the same week, at least two will have the same birthday? Round the answer to $3$ decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "Let $A$ denote the event that among $3$ people at least two have the same birthday. In this case, it is easier to find $P({A}^{′}),$ where ${A}^{′}$ represents the event that all three people have their birthdays on different days.  \n\nWe can compute the probability of the complement as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}^{′}) & =\\frac{n}{N},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the total number of ways to assign a day of the week to each person, and $n$ is the number of ways to assign a *unique* day of the week to each person.\n\nTo assign a unique day of the week to each of the $3$ people, we must choose $3$ days out of $7,$ where the order matters. The number of ways to do this is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n={}_{7}{P}_{3}=7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5=210.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, if the same day can be assigned to different persons, we have $7$ ways to assign a day for the first person, $7$ for the second and $7$ for the third person.  Multiplying these choices together, we get\n\n$$\nN=7\\cdot 7\\cdot 7=343.\n$$\n\nFinally, the probability of the complement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{¯}{A}) & =\\frac{n}{N}=\\frac{210}{343}≈0.612\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =1-P({A}^{′}) \\\\  & ≈1-0.612 \\\\  & =0.388\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
